[1] During World War II, the United States government urged Americans to grow some of their own food. [2] Commercial crops were needed to feed soldiers {{1|overseas in other countries}}, and canned goods were rationed at home. [3] The response was {{2|enormous; by 1944, an estimated}} 20 million victory gardens were growing in backyards, schoolyards, and vacant city lots. {{3|}}

[4] Gardening took some getting used to. [5] Many city dwellers had never planted a seed, {{4|yet}} newspapers, magazines, and government pamphlets offered advice on everything from soil to pests. [6] Community groups organized shared plots and classes, {{5|which met on weekends}}.

[7] The results were impressive. [8] By some estimates, victory gardens {{6|cranked out}} about 40 percent of the fresh vegetables eaten in the United States during the war. [9] {{7|Moreover,}} the gardens did more than supply food: they gave people at home a visible way to contribute to the war effort. [10] {{8|Many of the people who tended gardens they were children.}}

[11] After the war ended, most victory gardens disappeared as rationing ended and food became plentiful again. [12] {{9|Today, community gardens in many cities carry on the idea of growing food close to home.}}

[1] In late 1782, Joseph-Michel Montgolfier, the son of a paper manufacturer in southern France, noticed that a small fabric bag held over a fire {{1|rose toward the ceiling, he}} began to wonder whether a much larger bag could lift a person. [2] Working with his brother Jacques-Étienne, he built a series of larger balloons from paper and cloth.

[3] On June 4, 1783, the brothers {{2|showed people what they had made}} in their hometown of Annonay. [4] Word of the event reached Paris, and King Louis XVI asked to see a flight.

[5] On September 19, 1783, at the palace of Versailles, a balloon carried its first {{3|passengers, a sheep, a duck, and a rooster.}} [6] The animals landed safely about eight minutes later, which suggested that the air high above the ground was not {{4|totally deadly or anything}}. {{5|}}

[7] Two months later, on November 21, 1783, the science teacher Jean-François Pilâtre de Rozier and the Marquis d'Arlandes became the first people to fly in a free balloon. [8] {{6|Rising from a garden on the edge of Paris, the city spread out below them.}} [9] They drifted {{7|for a duration of about twenty-five minutes in length}} and landed some five miles away.

[10] {{8|Hydrogen is the lightest of all the chemical elements.}} [11] Only ten days later, the physicist Jacques Charles flew a balloon filled with hydrogen for about two hours. [12] {{9|Today, hot-air balloons are flown mainly for sport and sightseeing.}}
